WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.885
Most boxes in the storeroom still have their labels.

2
00:00:05.885 --> 00:00:09.990
Box of apples, box of lemons: the clerk at the door read every one.

3
00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:14.645
But one box sat by the back door through a wet week, and its label wore off.

4
00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:18.536
You can see that everything inside is the same kind of thing.

5
00:00:18.536 --> 00:00:20.341
You just can't see which kind.

6
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:23.180
So what can you safely do with it?

7
00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:26.227
Take something out and look at it, as some thing.

8
00:00:26.227 --> 00:00:27.470
Count what's inside.

9
00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:28.029
Empty it.

10
00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:30.330
What you can't do is put anything in.

11
00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:36.360
Whatever you drop might be the wrong kind: the lemon in the crate of apples, the other way round.

12
00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:40.185
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

13
00:00:40.185 --> 00:00:46.847
If I don't know the element type, why not just write MutableList&lt;Any?&gt;, the type that covers every value?

14
00:00:46.847 --> 00:00:49.639
Isn't that the same as saying, I don't know?

15
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:54.362
Let's start with a job where the type really doesn't matter.

16
00:00:54.362 --> 00:01:01.984
The shop keeps a daily log, and one helper prints lists into it: a list of variety names, a list of weights, a list of apples.

17
00:01:01.984 --> 00:01:04.705
It never needs to know what the elements are.

18
00:01:05.633 --> 00:01:12.243
Kotlin's way to say that is a star, in place of the type argument: List&lt;*&gt;.

19
00:01:12.243 --> 00:01:15.504
Java writes a question mark: List&lt;?&gt;.

20
00:01:15.504 --> 00:01:20.351
Each item comes out as Kotlin's Any?, or Java's Object.

21
00:01:20.351 --> 00:01:25.287
And the demo prints all five: Gala, Fuji, 180, 150, 120.

22
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:27.033
Let's map it.

23
00:01:27.033 --> 00:01:28.634
The worn box is the list.

24
00:01:28.634 --> 00:01:30.619
The worn-off label is the star.

25
00:01:30.619 --> 00:01:37.729
The one kind of thing inside is the list's real element type, which is still there: you've only lost the label.

26
00:01:37.729 --> 00:01:42.276
And taking something out to look at it is a read, which gives you Any?.

27
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.890
Now the interesting part.

28
00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:49.215
Take the list of names, and look at it through a MutableList&lt;*&gt;.

29
00:01:49.215 --> 00:01:53.337
Three lines: add the string Eureka, add null, clear the list.

30
00:01:53.337 --> 00:01:54.824
Which of them compile?

31
00:01:54.824 --> 00:01:55.905
Pause and guess.

32
00:01:59.100 --> 00:01:59.812
Only clear.

33
00:01:59.812 --> 00:02:05.312
Adding Eureka fails: the receiver type contains star projection, which prohibits add.

34
00:02:05.312 --> 00:02:10.619
The list might really be the weights, and no string may go into a list of numbers.

35
00:02:10.833 --> 00:02:11.497
And null?

36
00:02:11.497 --> 00:02:16.802
Refused too: null cannot be a value of a non-null type, CapturedType(*).

37
00:02:16.802 --> 00:02:20.634
The unknown type might be one that never holds null.

38
00:02:20.634 --> 00:02:22.918
Null gets in almost everywhere.

39
00:02:22.918 --> 00:02:23.581
Not here.

40
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:30.108
CapturedType(*) is the compiler's placeholder for the one real element type of this list.

41
00:02:30.108 --> 00:02:32.896
It's a name for the label nobody can read.

42
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.663
Java is more relaxed about one of these.

43
00:02:36.663 --> 00:02:42.318
On a List&lt;?&gt;, add null compiles, because every Java reference type allows null.

44
00:02:42.318 --> 00:02:45.252
The Java demo prints a list holding null.

45
00:02:45.252 --> 00:02:50.979
Adding the string Eureka fails in Java too: String cannot be converted to CAP#1.

46
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:57.499
Everything that doesn't take an element still works: size, isEmpty, removeAt, clear.

47
00:02:57.499 --> 00:02:59.898
So one function can empty any crate.

48
00:02:59.898 --> 00:03:07.429
The demo empties a list of names and a list of weights, and prints: first is Gala, emptied 2 names and 3 weights.

49
00:03:08.333 --> 00:03:10.329
Now back to the shy question.

50
00:03:10.329 --> 00:03:12.119
Why not MutableList&lt;Any?&gt;?

51
00:03:12.119 --> 00:03:13.495
Let's be fair to it.

52
00:03:13.495 --> 00:03:17.831
It's a perfectly real type: the shop's drawer of odds and ends.

53
00:03:17.831 --> 00:03:20.722
A pencil, a number, nothing in particular.

54
00:03:20.722 --> 00:03:23.200
You can put anything at all into it.

55
00:03:24.133 --> 00:03:29.314
So hand a function that takes a MutableList&lt;Any?&gt; our list of names.

56
00:03:29.314 --> 00:03:32.056
Kotlin says: argument type mismatch.

57
00:03:32.056 --> 00:03:37.465
Actual type is MutableList&lt;String&gt;, but MutableList&lt;Any?&gt; was expected.

58
00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:39.385
Think about why.

59
00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:45.116
A drawer that takes anything would let that function add a price tag to our list of names.

60
00:03:45.116 --> 00:03:52.310
So the type that looks the most generous is the fussiest: it only takes a list that really is a list of anything.

61
00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:54.157
The worn box is the opposite.

62
00:03:54.157 --> 00:03:56.576
Nothing may go in, so every list fits.

63
00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:58.419
Now the name.

64
00:03:58.419 --> 00:04:02.874
A star in place of a type argument is called a star projection.

65
00:04:02.874 --> 00:04:10.299
And Java calls its question mark an unbounded wildcard: a wildcard with no extends and no super after it.

66
00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.392
Here the worn label picture breaks.

67
00:04:13.392 --> 00:04:19.341
In the storeroom, you could open the box, look at one apple, and know what the whole box holds.

68
00:04:19.341 --> 00:04:20.342
A program can't.

69
00:04:20.342 --> 00:04:25.164
An empty list, or one that starts with an apple, says nothing about the rest.

70
00:04:25.164 --> 00:04:27.105
The star just means: not known.

71
00:04:28.033 --> 00:04:29.245
So, the guardrail.

72
00:04:29.245 --> 00:04:33.956
If a function doesn't care about the element type, say so with a star.

73
00:04:33.956 --> 00:04:36.783
It may take items out, and put nothing in.

74
00:04:36.783 --> 00:04:42.033
Write MutableList&lt;Any?&gt; only when the function really stores arbitrary values.

75
00:04:42.933 --> 00:04:46.851
So, is a list of anything the same as a list of I don't know?

76
00:04:46.851 --> 00:04:47.044
No.

77
00:04:47.044 --> 00:04:51.540
The drawer says what it holds, anything at all, so anything may go in.

78
00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:56.613
The worn box holds one kind of thing that nobody remembers, so nothing goes in.

79
00:04:56.833 --> 00:04:57.821
Quick check.

80
00:04:57.821 --> 00:05:00.292
unlabeled is a MutableList&lt;*&gt;.

81
00:05:00.292 --> 00:05:03.915
Does unlabeled dot removeAt of zero compile?

82
00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:07.377
Yes.

83
00:05:07.377 --> 00:05:10.144
removeAt takes an index, not an element.

84
00:05:10.144 --> 00:05:15.056
A star projection locks what goes in, not whether the crate can change.
